Let the handler refuse a TCP connection before the gVisor stack completes the
handshake.

keqdroid's mihomo on Android rejects connections from apps outside the VPN
(mihomo-uid-owner-guard.patch): such an app can bind a socket to tun0 with no
rights since Linux 5.7. The rule ran only after the forwarder had already
answered the SYN, so the app saw a connection open and then close, which is
proof that the tun answers. A handler that implements TCPRejecter is now asked
first and a refusal gets a reset. A handler without the method sees no change.

The answer comes from mihomo-uid-owner-handshake.patch.

Applied to github.com/metacubex/sing-tun at the version mihomo's go.mod pins,
by tool/build_mihomo.ps1, which points mihomo at the patched copy.

diff --git a/stack_gvisor_tcp.go b/stack_gvisor_tcp.go
--- a/stack_gvisor_tcp.go
+++ b/stack_gvisor_tcp.go
@@ -77,7 +77,23 @@ func (f *TCPForwarder) HandlePacket(id stack.TransportEndpointID, pkt *stack.Pac
 	return f.forwarder.HandlePacket(id, pkt)
 }
 
+// TCPRejecter is a handler that may refuse a connection before the handshake.
+// keqdroid, see tool/patches/sing-tun-reject-before-handshake.patch.
+type TCPRejecter interface {
+	RejectTCPBeforeHandshake(source M.Socksaddr, destination M.Socksaddr) bool
+}
+
 func (f *TCPForwarder) Forward(r *tcp.ForwarderRequest) {
+	if rejecter, ok := f.handler.(TCPRejecter); ok {
+		id := r.ID()
+		if rejecter.RejectTCPBeforeHandshake(
+			M.SocksaddrFrom(AddrFromAddress(id.RemoteAddress), id.RemotePort),
+			M.SocksaddrFrom(AddrFromAddress(id.LocalAddress), id.LocalPort),
+		) {
+			r.Complete(true)
+			return
+		}
+	}
 	var wq waiter.Queue
 	var cancel context.CancelFunc
 	if parentDone := f.ctx.Done(); parentDone != nil {
